// only 46% inward, which reads as a cloud changing in place rather than air
// entering through a window. Two changes fix that:
//
//   · the direction is built from the window's own basis (see TyndallEffect),
//     so ≥75% of it points along the window normal by construction;
//   · the noise is sampled in that frame with DIFFERENT scales across and along
//     the flow. Stretching features along the direction of travel is what makes
//     motion legible — an isotropic blob looks the same after it has moved.
//
// ── …and why the two scale sliders both only change the GRAIN ──────────────
// Everything in this function is spatial. \`uScaleAcross\` sets how big a wisp is
// and \`uScaleAlong\` how stretched it is; neither decides whether the air looks
// ALIVE. That is set by two things outside it:
//
//   · \`uAirSpeed\`, through tau = (1/scaleAcross)/airSpeed — the seconds a wisp
//     takes to travel its own width. At the pre-v3.6 default that was 12.5 s,
//     far below the threshold where motion is perceptible, so the field was
//     technically moving and looked painted on. No combination of the two scale
//     sliders could fix that, which is why they both just felt like "grain".
//   · \`uWarpAmount\`, which is what actually makes features CHANGE SHAPE as they
//     travel rather than sliding rigidly.
//
// The field's own reorganisation was never missing — the two octaves advect
// along the same direction at different rates (1.0 and 1.43), and their beat
// continuously rebuilds the sum. Measured as "best correlation after the ideal
// re-alignment", i.e. shape change with translation factored out:
//
//     speed 0.032   t+1s 0.998   t+3s 0.979   t+5s 0.927   t+8s 0.83
//     speed 0.100   t+1s 0.977   t+3s 0.800   t+5s 0.486   t+8s 0.198
